1:1-1 (* EXPECT-ERROR: foreign procedure must declare [SERIAL] or [REENTRANT] *)
2:1-13 (* This is the blosc trap: a global-state C function raced across\n   worker threads and answered right twelve times.  The language's\n   answer is the ATTRIBUTE.  Every foreign procedure says [SERIAL] or\n   [REENTRANT], since an unstated contract cannot be checked.  The\n   generator brackets every call of a [SERIAL] one with its unit's\n   monitor, so threads wait (decision 8; runtime/test/serialtest.m9\n   shows [REENTRANT] losing 85% of the updates where [SERIAL] is\n   exact).  Until 2026-10-08 this piece expected `SERIAL procedure\n   called from THREAD context'.  That was a per-unit chain check from\n   before the emitted monitor, and it refused exactly what the monitor\n   makes correct.  The declaration that says nothing is what must not\n   compile. *)
comments=2
